# REQUIRES: clang, llvm-as, llvm-dis, x86-target
#
# BITCODE CANONICALISATION AND THE FREEZE DISCIPLINE.
#
# This is the .bc level, and it is the level the rev-4 freeze story is actually
# about. T is defined as "the canonical reparsed LLVM module": serialize to
# bitcode B, record SHA-256(B), destroy the mutable module, parse B in a fresh
# context, and confirm the parsed module serialises to the same hash. NFConforms
# clause 2 is exactly that hash equality.
#
# THE NON-OBVIOUS FACT THIS PINS. With the recorded LLVM 17 producer, the
# bitcode Clang emits is not its own canonical form. One normalisation round
# trip is required, after which serialisation is idempotent:
#
#   b0 = clang -c -emit-llvm ...      # emitted
#   b1 = llvm-as(llvm-dis(b0))        # canonical      b1 != b0
#   b2 = llvm-as(llvm-dis(b1))        # fixpoint       b2 == b1
#
# WHY AN IMPLEMENTER NEEDS THIS. On a producer whose direct output is not a
# writer fixpoint, hashing that output and later comparing it against a
# reparse-and-reserialise will fail. The profile is careful to say the reparsed
# module must serialise to the same hash "under the canonical writer." The
# generic test below establishes the fixpoint; the companion LLVM-17 test pins
# the concrete producer that needs the initial normalization.
#
# These checks make no ModelStatus claim.

# --- emitted bitcode, and its canonical form -------------------------------
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -O2 -c -emit-llvm %harness/c/argmax_release_body.c -o %t.b0.bc
# RUN: %llvm-dis %t.b0.bc -o %t.b0.ll
# RUN: %llvm-as %t.b0.ll -o %t.b1.bc

# --- one more round trip: serialisation is now idempotent -------------------
# RUN: %llvm-dis %t.b1.bc -o %t.b1.ll
# RUN: %llvm-as %t.b1.ll -o %t.b2.bc
# RUN: cmp -s %t.b1.bc %t.b2.bc

# The b1 == b2 fixpoint is the toolchain-independent content of "canonical".
# The LLVM-17-specific b0 != b1 producer observation lives in the companion
# bitcode-producer-normalization.test rather than skipping this generic check
# on every newer toolchain.

# --- the reparsed module still says what it said ----------------------------
# Canonicalisation must not change the facts the audit reads. The argmax body
# is inside the accepted integer surface before and after.
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=CANON --input-file=%t.b1.ll
# CANON-NOT: @llvm.fsh
# CANON-DAG: icmp
# CANON-DAG: select i1

# --- the same discipline on a module that is OUTSIDE the fragment -----------
# Canonicalisation is not a filter. A module the audit must reject still
# round-trips cleanly and still contains the rejected construct afterwards, so a
# funnel shift cannot be laundered by a serialise/parse cycle.
# RUN: %clang -std=c11 -Wall -Wextra -Wpedantic -fno-builtin --target=x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu -O2 -c -emit-llvm %harness/c/sha256_round_release_body.c -o %t.h0.bc
# RUN: %llvm-dis %t.h0.bc -o %t.h0.ll
# RUN: %llvm-as %t.h0.ll -o %t.h1.bc
# RUN: %llvm-dis %t.h1.bc -o %t.h1.ll
# RUN: %llvm-as %t.h1.ll -o %t.h2.bc
# RUN: cmp -s %t.h1.bc %t.h2.bc
# RUN: %FileCheck %s --check-prefix=CANON-HASH --input-file=%t.h1.ll
# CANON-HASH: llvm.fshl.i32
